The git init command turns the current folder into a repository. It creates the .git folder and nothing else.A repository is a folder that Git is tracking. It is a normal folder plus one hidden subfolder named .git, where Git stores the entire history. Folder plus history, together in one place.

The git status command reports what Git currently sees in your repository: which files are new, which are changed, and which are ready to go into the next snapshot. It changes nothing. You will run it constantly, before and after almost every other command

The staging area is a waiting room between your folder and the recorded history. Before Git makes a commit, you first place files into the staging area with git add. Only what is staged goes into the commit.
The file moved from Untracked files to Changes to be committed. That heading is Git's name for the staging area's contents: this is what the next snapshot will contain. git add prints nothing when it succeeds. git status is how you see the result.

Ex: git add day2.txt
git status --short

Undo git add .
The command git restore --staged <file> takes a file back out of the staging area. The file itself is not touched: its content stays exactly as it is in your folder. Only its place in the staging area changes.

A commit is a saved snapshot of your staged files, stored permanently inside the repository. Once you make a commit, that version of your work is recorded and you can return to it at any time.
A commit records the exact content of every staged file at that moment, plus the date it was made. Git stores it inside .git, and the commit never changes after it is made.

Every commit stores four things:

The snapshot. The full content of every staged file at that moment.
The message. A short sentence you write that says what changed and why.
The author. Your name and email, taken from the user.name and user.email settings you configured earlier.
A pointer to the parent. Each commit remembers which commit came before it. This is how Git builds a chain of history, one commit after another.

