Every diff is built from three kinds of lines:

Lines starting with - are the old version. They exist in the first snapshot but not the second.
Lines starting with + are the new version.
Lines starting with a space are unchanged. Git prints a few of them around each change so you can see where in the file it happened.

Three types of git diff
git diff compares working files against the staging area. It answers: what have I edited but not staged yet?
git diff --cached compares the staging area against HEAD. It answers: what will my next commit contain?
git diff HEAD compares working files against HEAD. It answers: what changed in total since the last commit?

The one to remember before every commit is --cached. It shows the exact content of the commit you are about to make, so you can check it before the snapshot is recorded.

