Ignoring files 

Git reads a plain text file named .gitignore in the top folder of your repository. Each line is a pattern. Any untracked file that matches a pattern disappears from git status and is skipped by git add ..

EX:
app.log
secrets.env
build/


A .gitignore pattern only affects untracked files. If a file was already committed, Git keeps tracking it, and adding its name to .gitignore changes nothing. One command fixes that situation.

Untracking a committed junk file takes three steps:

Add the file's name to .gitignore, so it never comes back.
Run git rm --cached <file> to take it off the tracking list.
Commit. This commit records the removal, and from then on the file is invisible to Git.
After the commit, git status is clean, the file still exists on disk, and git check-ignore <file> confirms Git now ignores it.


BRANCH 

Git does not copy your files when you create a branch. A branch is only a small pointer, a name attached to one commit. Creating one is instant and costs almost nothing, even in a huge repository. That is why Git users create branches for everything: one branch per bug fix, per feature, per experiment.

Two commands do the basics. 
git branch <name> creates a branch, and 
git branch with no name lists them:


$ git switch new-menu
Switched to branch 'new-menu'

You will usually want to start a branch and move onto it immediately. The -c flag (short for create) does both:
$ git switch -c fix-hours
Switched to a new branch 'fix-hours'

Your commit history is a chain of commits, each one linked to the one before it. A branch is a named pointer that stores the id of one commit. main is a pointer. new-menu is a pointer. Committing on a branch adds one commit to the chain and updates that branch's pointer to the new commit's id. The other pointers do not change.

git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all ---> Seeing all branches at once 


Merge
Both branches started from the same commit and then grew their own commits. A merge combines those two lines of commits into one, so a single branch ends up holding the changes made on both sides.

A merge always has a direction. You stand on the branch that should RECEIVE the work, and you name the branch that HAS the work:

git switch main
git merge add-search

This reads as: "I am on main. Pull the commits from add-search into main." The add-search branch itself is not changed by this. Only main moves.


What you need before merging
Two habits keep merges safe:

Run git status first. Merging with uncommitted changes in your working directory can mix them into the result.
Run git log --oneline on both branches so you know what is about to come in.



The two kinds of merge
By default, Git picks one of two behaviors automatically, based on the shape of the history:

Fast-forward merge. The receiving branch has no new commits of its own. Git simply moves the receiving branch's pointer forward to the tip of the other branch. No new commit is created.
Note: It simply moves the pointer to the commit that the recieving branch has  

Three-way merge -- When both branches have new commits, git cannot just slide a label forward. It performs a three-way merge: it compares both branch tips against their common starting point, combines the changes, and records the result in a new commit. That new commit is a merge commit.

(Every non-root normal commit has one parent: the commit that came before it. In this usual two-branch merge, the merge commit has TWO parents:

The first parent is the tip of the branch you were standing on.
The second parent is the tip of the branch you merged in.)



git switch main
git merge drinks -m "Merge drinks into main"  --> The -m flag gives the merge commit its message

Ex: 
*   9f3c2ab Merge drinks into main
|\
| * 71d0e4c Add drinks list
* | 4b8a911 Add desserts list
|/
* 2c5f7d0 Start the menu


