Merge conflicts 

<<<<<<< HEAD starts YOUR side: the version from the branch you were standing on when you ran the merge. This side is often called ours.
======= is the divider between the two versions.
>>>>>>> sam-slogan ends THEIR side: the version from the branch you merged in. This side is called theirs. The branch name after the marker tells you where it came from.
Everything outside the markers merged cleanly and needs no attention. Only the marked block is in dispute.

To resolve, open the file in an editor and rewrite the marked block into the final text you want
After editing, tell git the file is settled, and finish the merge with a commit:

bash

git add slogan.md
git commit -m "Merge sam-slogan, combine both slogans"



Taking one whole side
Sometimes you do not want to mix. One side is simply right for a given file. Git can pick a whole side for you:

bash

git checkout --theirs config.yaml
git checkout --ours README.md
git add config.yaml README.md
--theirs replaces the file with the incoming branch's version, and --ours with your branch's version. You still git add the files and commit to finish, the same as with hand editing.


The git merge --abort command cancels a conflicted merge and returns the repository to the exact state it had before you ran git merge. Nothing from the attempted merge remains.



UNDOING CHANGES
git restore throws away a bad edit in your working files. Nothing was committed yet. This is the gentlest undo.
(Two warnings
The edit is gone for good. Git may still have committed or staged versions, but it cannot bring back an edit that existed only in the working directory before you restored over it.
It works per tracked path. git restore . discards edits in tracked files under the current directory. It does not remove untracked files. Prefer naming the exact file you want to reset.)

git commit --amend fixes the last commit you made: a typo in the message, or a file you forgot to include.
(You use it for two everyday mistakes:
You made a typo in the commit message.
You forgot to include a file in the commit.

Amending does not stack a new commit on top. It takes the last commit apart, adds your correction, and seals a fresh commit in its place. The commit count stays the same, but the commit gets a new hash, because a commit's hash is a fingerprint of its content.)

git reset moves your branch back to an earlier commit, removing the last few commits from that branch's visible history.

((The three modes
--soft: the branch moves back. All the abandoned changes stay staged, ready to commit again.
--mixed (the default): the branch moves back. The abandoned changes stay in your working files, but unstaged.
--hard: the branch moves back and tracked changes are discarded from the staging area and working files. Untracked files normally remain.)
)

git revert adds a new commit that cancels out an old one. The old commit stays in history.
(This is the big difference from reset:

reset erases commits from your branch. History gets shorter.
revert cancels a commit with a fresh one. History gets longer by one commit, and the old commit is not removed.

Anyone reading the log later sees both the mistake and the fix: the bad commit stays where it was, and the revert commit on top records exactly when and how it was cancelled.)

Ex: git revert --no-edit 7c3d902
git log --oneline

The --no-edit flag accepts the automatic message (Revert "...") without opening an editor. The bad change is gone from the files, the bad commit is still in the log, and the revert commit sits on top.

NOTE:
restore, amend, and reset rewrite or discard work. They are safe while the work is only on your machine. --> Rewrites history 
revert adds to history. It does not remove the old commit from history, so it is usually the safe choice once a commit has been shared.

git reflog 
IT lists every commit your branches have pointed at recently, including the ones an undo dropped, so a commit you removed by mistake is usually still findable for a while. It is a rescue tool, not a plan: treat every undo as final and use the reflog only when one goes wrong.


STASH & TAGS
The git stash command saves your tracked uncommitted changes inside the repository and gives you a clean working tree. Untracked files need git stash -u. Later, git stash pop reapplies the changes to your working tree; add --index when you also need the original staged state restored.

You need this when urgent work interrupts unfinished work. You are halfway through an edit, it is nowhere near ready to commit, and suddenly a production bug needs a fix on a clean tree. You stash the half-finished edit, commit the fix on the clean tree, then pop the stash and continue where you stopped.

COmmands:
git stash saves tracked uncommitted changes (staged and unstaged) and cleans the working tree. Use -u for untracked files.
git stash list shows every stash entry you have saved.
git stash pop re-applies the most recent stash to your files and removes it from the list when it applies cleanly.

